WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
Welcome back to Models and Agents episode 32.

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.260
It's April 17th, 2026.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.800
The AI world never sleeps.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.180
Here's what you need to know today.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:12.840
Quinn 3.6 to 35ba3b brings Sparse MoE Vision language

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.480
capabilities with only 3 billion active parameters

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.380
and strong, agentic coding performance.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.260
The Twin Team just opens source to model

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.660
that changes the math on what you can run locally

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.160
for multimodal agent work.

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.200
This is the top story today because it delivers serious coding

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.720
and vision performance while keeping inference costs

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.680
remarkably low.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.300
The model has 35 billion total parameters

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.760
but activates just 3 billion per forward pass

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.440
thanks to a sparse mixture of experts' architecture.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.060
That design gives it better coding performance

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.880
than dense models in the 27 to 31 billion parameter range

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.960
while using memory and compute much closer to smaller models.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.400
Early community testing shows it shines

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.940
at Python data analysis, building web apps

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.420
and image recognition tasks.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.260
It does have a noticeable gap in non-English performance

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.200
compared to its English and Chinese results

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.420
so keep that in mind for multilingual work.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.260
For developers, this means you can now run capable

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.340
vision language agents on modest GPUs

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.080
that previously demanded much larger dense models.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.160
If you have been building coding assistance,

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.240
document understanding tools or multimodal agents,

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.580
this release is worth testing immediately.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.240
The open source nature plus expected quantized versions

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.720
should make integration into popular inference engines

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.240
straight forward in the coming days.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.160
This release really strengthens the open source

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.600
ecosystems ability to compete with closed

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.580
multimodal models on practical agent tasks.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.680
Now on the model update side,

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.980
the community is buzzing about ternary bonsai

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:39.800
from PrismML.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.040
They released a family of models at 8 billion,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.580
4 billion, and 1.7 billion parameters

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.260
using ternary weights of negative 1, 0, and positive 1.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.000
These models achieve roughly nine times

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.040
smaller memory footprint than FP16 equivalents

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.080
while outperforming peers in their size class

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.180
on standard benchmarks.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.780
The models come in FP16 safety sores

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.580
for immediate hugging face compatibility,

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:06.460
and MLX two-bit packed formats are already supported.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.220
People are especially excited about the roadmap

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:12.380
toward 20 to 40 billion parameter ternary models

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.960
that could challenge today's leading dense and MOE releases.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.220
For anyone doing edge or memory constrained deployments,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.220
this feels like a practical step forward

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.940
where every bit truly matters.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:26.880
Another notable release is Sage Sealer 2.6

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.300
introduced in 5 billion, 10 billion, and 27 billion sizes.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.880
The team added architectural modifications

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:37.080
inverse reasoning training to reduce cascading errors

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.800
and a native end-to-end vision encoder.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.180
These models emphasize strong mathematics, coding,

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.120
and general intelligence alongside dedicated optimization

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.780
for South Asian languages using a custom Devoniguri tokenizer.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.760
What stands out is how they balance

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.360
English reasoning capability with competitive performance

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.920
in Nepali and Hindi without the usual multilingual degradation.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.460
The native multimodal design avoids the common adaptor pitfalls

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.840
that plague many other vision language efforts.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.080
Researchers also published a systematic comparison

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.860
of linguistic adaptation across comparable sized

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.060
large language models.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.920
They tested Lama 3.1 to 8B,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.940
Mestral 7B dash V0.1,

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.820
and Quinn 3 to 8B on Romanized Nepali

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:23.440
using a 10,000 sample data set under both 0 shot

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.320
and Qlora fine-tuned conditions.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.720
After fine-tuning with RS-lora at rank 32,

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.340
all models improved dramatically

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.480
with Quinn 3 to 8B leading most structural metrics.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.280
Lama 3.1 to 8B showed the largest gains

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.360
from its relatively weak 0 shot baseline.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.960
This work gives the first rigorous baseline

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:44.620
for this under-resourced informal digital language

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.920
and confirms there is still substantial headroom

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.920
for low resource adaptation pipelines.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:51.540
Shifting to agent and tool developments

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.720
of practitioner on the machine learning subreddit

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.440
is looking for collaborators on a chaos monkey framework

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:57.980
for multi-agent systems.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.220
After hitting production issues with multi-agent setups,

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.400
they built a basic stress testing tool

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.820
and want to mature it into a proper benchmarking

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:06.920
and reliability suite.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.800
If you have shipped multi-agent systems at scale,

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.820
this is a direct opportunity to contribute domain expertise

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.220
and help establish better evaluation practices

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:16.720
for production agent reliability.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:18.580
On the research side,

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:21.460
the H-technique rag paper introduces a two-stage

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:24.460
hierarchical retrieval system for cyber threat intelligence.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.040
It first identifies miter attack tactics

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:28.960
than narrows to specific techniques,

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.600
cutting the candidate search space by 77.5%.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.640
The approach adds tactic aware, re-ranking

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.900
and hierarchy constrained context organization.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:42.720
The result is a 3.8% F1 improvement over prior technique rag

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.360
while reducing inference latency by 62.4%

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.220
and LLMAPI calls by 60%.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.820
Security teams working on automated cyber threat

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:54.920
intelligence mapping should evaluate this

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.620
for both accuracy gains and significant cost savings.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.460
In the practical and community space,

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.640
one local Yama contributor posted a detailed head-to-head

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:06.320
of Quinn 3.6 versus Gemma 4 models.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.240
The comparison covers text review, grammar checking

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.040
and social sciences, light Python data analysis

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.160
and JavaScript typescript web work.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.220
Chwen leads on coding, stem reasoning

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.460
and image recognition while Gemma offers better

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.080
non-English flexibility and more fuzzy creative thinking

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.740
suited to humanity's tasks.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.120
Both models still show some political and cultural biases

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:27.960
though improved from earlier versions

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.280
and the author even finds the occasional hallucination

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:32.340
useful for staying mentally engaged.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.780
The post serves as a practical field guide

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:36.180
for choosing between the two families

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.980
based on your specific use case.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:41.300
Elsewhere, a machine learning engineer asked the community

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.740
whether to buy an M5 series Mac with MLX

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:47.860
or build a custom RTX 5,090 machine.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.100
Their workload breaks down to 70% fine-tuning

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:54.460
large pre-trained models and 30% training from scratch,

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.780
mostly image and video with occasional large language models.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:00.680
The discussion delivers useful real world insights

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.220
on VRAM needs versus Apple's improving

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:06.300
MLX ecosystem for on-device and fine-tuning work.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:09.140
It is a timely look at the budget, memory

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:10.980
and framework maturity trade-offs

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.740
many practitioners face right now.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.220
Researchers also released Memground,

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.220
a long-term memory evaluation kit

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.700
for large language models and gamified scenarios.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:22.380
It uses a three-tier hierarchical benchmark

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.760
covering surface state, temporal associative

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:28.740
and reasoning based memory inside rich interactive environments

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.560
instead of static retrieval tests.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:33.300
The suite adds multi-dimensional metrics

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:34.860
like memory fragments unlocked

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.740
and exploration trajectory diagrams.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:39.720
Current state-of-the-art models and memory agents

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.620
still struggle with dynamic tracking

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.360
and long-term evidence reasoning according to these tests.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.720
Teams building long-context agents

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:49.660
or memory-augmented systems now have a much more realistic

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.960
evaluation suite to measure real progress.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:56.420
Okay, let's pop the hood on these post-transformer adapters

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:59.100
for logic correction that the community has been discussing.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.340
Everyone talks about uncensoring or fixing aligned models

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.140
as if you simply need better prompts or bigger models.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:08.500
In practice, the suppression of factual log probabilities

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.420
on sensitive topics often lives in the final layers

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:14.380
even when the knowledge is clearly present in hidden states.

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:18.760
The elegant engineering fix is a tiny post-transformer adapter

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.760
of roughly 786,000 parameters,

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:26.820
which is only 0.02% of a 4 billion to 14 billion parameter

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:27.480
base model.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.500
It reads frozen hidden states

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:30.760
and learns a corrective mapping

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.840
before the final language model head.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:35.260
This restores proper probability rankings

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:37.220
without retraining the entire model.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:40.500
Both gated Swig-LU and simple linear bottleneck versions

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.460
perform comparably so the exact architecture seems

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.040
less critical than the intervention point itself.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:48.840
Applying the adapter at every token position

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:50.820
during generation destroys coherence.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:53.440
Restricting it to the current prediction position

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.160
meaning less token only yields fluent and less censored text.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.600
A logic space adapter after token projection fails

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:03.240
entirely confirming that hidden state intervention

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.240
is the correct level of abstraction.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:07.580
Training uses anchored examples

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:09.900
to prevent regression on general knowledge.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:13.220
The adapter delivers perfect memorization of training facts

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:17.100
in 11 to 39% generalization to held out items

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.340
across different scales.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:22.340
The quality gain is most pronounced on smaller models.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:25.320
Above roughly 70 billion parameters,

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.940
the hidden state misalignment appears to shrink

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.380
and the adapter's relative value drops.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:32.980
So when should you actually reach for this

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:34.100
versus the alternative?

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.380
Use post transformer adapters

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.460
when you need surgical behavior correction

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:39.800
on a frozen base model

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:42.720
and can afford a few hundred thousand extra parameters.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.220
They are dramatically cheaper than full fine tuning

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:48.300
or preference optimization for targeted logit repair.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:50.620
The gotcha that still bites teams

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:52.780
is applying the adapter at the wrong position

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:53.900
in the generation loop.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.040
Always validate last position only behavior first.

